In article <87u1r4a4w0.fsf@esoteric.holy-unicorn> Markus Kliegl <markus.kliegl@t-online.de> writes:
>I'm trying to install 5.1A (or, failing that, 4.0F) on a DEC 2000 AXP 500.
>Currently 4.0F is installed, but the system is in a fairly inconsistent state
>and needs to be completely reinstalled. The problem I'm facing is booting
>from the CD drive at the SRM console. 'show dev' tells me that the CD drive is
>DKA500, which is awkward, as I have been told that this should normally be
>DKA400. Typing in 'boot dka500' gives me
>
>INIT-S-CPU...
>AUDIT_BOOT_STARTS ...
>
>  84 FAIL
>
>I get this message, regardless of any BOOT_OSFLAGS settings, and what CD, if
>any, I have in the drive. Under the currently installed Tru64, however,
>everything works just fine (I can mount and read CDs). The same holds for the
>ARC console, which will gladly read the firmware updates CD.
>
>Is something misconfigured at the SRM console that I could fix?
>Or is there maybe a way to start a complete reinstall from the CD mounted
>under Tru64?

This is almost certainly an attempt to boot off a disk that is not
labelled with a proper bootblock. If V4.0F was installed on it (and
it was a boot disk), then the disk has become corrupt or damaged.

You can put the disk on another system and force boot blocks to it,
but that may or may not help if other things are damaged. Your best
bet is to reinstall from CD, that will relabel the disk correctly.

