>| But I want it to be dsk0, I do not know how to do.

The content of the disklabel isn't likely to be the problem.  But
if you want to change it some part of the label, use the disklabel
command to edit the label as you need.

What you're most likely running up against is that in V5, devices
names are not directly associated with the physical address of the
device as they were in V4 and earlier.  There, the SCSI bus address
determine the name.  You moved a device and its name changed.  This
had serious problems when you started adding new SCSI bus adapters
that appeared "between" two existing ones.

In V5, names are assigned as disks are discovered.  The first disk
found will be dsk0, the 2nd dsk1 and so on.  Left to itself, parallel
SCSI disks are uniquely identified by their serial number (expected
to be in one of the Inquiry pages).  Fibre Channel disks just use
their world-wide name.  This allows a given disk to retain the same
name no matter what its "address" is.

So, you have two potential problems:

o  The new disk is named dsk1 instead of dsk0 (unrelated to the label).
o  The kernel remembers that its system disk was dsk0.

As I said above, I believe there are solutions to these problems,
probably around changing the hardware database on the new system
disk.  They may be documented somewhere, but I haven't kept track
of where that document is, or how to make the change.

