In article <5Kxl8.4116$523.327014@ozemail.com.au>,
 "Rajanish Calisa" <rcalisa_nospam@ozemail.com.au> wrote:

X Hi all,
X 
X We have an application that is compiled and runs on Tru64 4.0F. Our client
X who is using this
X applications wants to upgrade to Tru64 5.1. I want to know if 5.1 can also
X run 4.0F binaries.
X If not, is there a FAQ in 4.0F to 5.1 migration from a user as well as a
X programmer's perspective?

Forward compatibility is an intentional goal for Tru64 UNIX versions.  
Programs from the very first release of Tru64 UNIX (aka Digital UNIX, 
aka OSF/1 from Digital Equipment Corporation), still run on the current 
release.

The exceptions tend to be code that is compiled into the kernel, such as 
device drivers, 3rd party file systems, etc...  In this case, the 
preferred goal to to just require a recompile of the kernel specific 
code.  _AND_ the goal is to only allow this to happen during a major 
release.

A great deal of work goes into any release to make sure that existing 
programs work on a new release.  If a normal program fails to work on a 
new release, then this may be a binary compatibility problem and should 
be escalated.

                                        Bob Harris


