According to the above, only about 60% of shareholders cast votes.  If true,
and the company needed 50% 'yea' votes of the total shares held (not just
voted) to pass the merger, then it's not entirely clear that the deal
'breezed through' (e.g., anything under a 5 to 1 margin wouldn't have been
sufficient to pass it).

It also states, "Capellas called the merger the highlight of his career."
How true:  avoiding being fired for incompetence and locking in a
substantial bonus in the bargain just has to feel good.

>
>    It generally was expected that the deal would breeze through the vote
>    by Compaq shareholders because of the premium HP has agreed to pay for
>    the company. At last count, the deal was valued at roughly $21
>    billion, compared with Compaq's recent market capitalization of
>    roughly $18.3 billion.

That reasoning would seem to apply to those Compaq shareholders anxious to
unload ASAP at the highest price possible (though FAIK that could be 90% of
them - they certainly have reason), or to those who believe that *any* new
leadership, regardless of how difficult the merger turns out to be, will be
an improvement with the potential to recover some substantial portion of the
inherent value of the company that's been lost under the current honchos.
The only reasons Q shareholders (at least those without significant HP
holdings as well) would have rejected the merger would have been  a)
confidence in the current leadership (yeah, right) or  b) the hope that
rejection would result in replacement of the current leadership (but under
the existing BoD there's little reason to believe that this would
necessarily improve things).

Since I'm no market guru, does anyone out there who is (i.e., who has a lot
more experience, not just is readier to make a wild guess) know what to look
for now?

It seems clear that if both stocks remain in the doldrums relative to the
rest of the market (they certainly fell more today than either the Dow or
the NASD), that indicates low expectations for the viability of the merger.
But are there any particular conclusions to be drawn if, e.g., Compaq shares
continue to trade below what the merger offer values them at?  I suppose
that the more Compaq looks like an absolute dog (e.g., when the Q1 numbers
come out), the more there's the possibility that HP's BoD will revolt and
scotch the deal, even at the cost of $675 million, but given how much like
complete idiots that would make HP's board look (compared with hanging tough
and hoping for a miracle, fiduciary duties be damned) that seems, to say the
least, unlikely.

- bill





