In article <Pine.GSO.4.10.10203120124140.1093-100000@trillian.livewire.ca>,
Dennis Clarke  <dclarke@livewire.ca> wrote:
> 4.2 BSD ( 1983 )  ->  4.3 BSD ( 1986 ) -> 4.3 BSD Tahoe ( 1988 )

	Most "classic" commercial UNIXes were based on 4.2: SunOS through
	SunOS 4, and HPUX, for example.

> The 4.3 BSD Tahoe release spawned two derivitives : 

>    1)  BSD Networking Software Release 1.0 (1989) : Net/1

	This is sort of a dead end as far as the OS goes, but it established
	precedent for Net/2 and 4.4-Lite.

>    2)  4.3 BSD Reno ( 1990 ) which seems to be a natural 
>        progression of the BSD implementation

	Tru64 is based on 4.3-Reno.

> From the 4.3 BSD Reno we see another revision of BSD Networking Software
> Release 2.0 (1991) : Net/2.

	This led to 386BSD and the first release of BSDOS.

	386BSD led to FreeBSD 1.x, and NetBSD.

> 4.4 BSD ( 1993 ) resulted in two flavours of BSD Lite : 

>    1)  4.4 BSD-Lite (1994) referred to as Net/3 
>    2)  4.4 BSD-Lite2 (1995)

	FreeBSD 2.0 was a fresh project based on 4.4-Lite.
	NetBSD (and OpenBSD, IIRC) seem to have evolved by merging
		4.4-Lite into the code base.

> Now where do the SVR4 flavours fit in?

	In the beginning with Version 6.

	Well, there were 5 editions before that, but the last time all
UNIX looked more or less the same (ignoring things like MERT) was the
6th Edition, released to the world as Version 6. So...

	In the beginning was Version 6.

	And AT&T (information systems) looked on Version 6, and saw that it
was good, and from Version 6 and the Programmwers Work Bench (PWB) developed
System III, which was to become the One True Commercial UNIX.

	And AT&T (Bell Labs) continued their own development, with the 7th
and 8th editions, all the way to the 10th edition, and then they decided it
was time for a change and they created Plan 9.

	And Microsoft looked upon Version 6, and saw that it was good, and
developed Xenix. And then they looked upon System III and saw that it was
good, and developed Xenix version 3. And then they decided that they would
never have a monopoly on UNIX and spun of SCO, who developed Xenix Version 2
(a different version) based on Version 7 UNIX... and we will say more of SCO
later.

	And Berkeley looked upon Version 6, and saw that it was good, and
released a set of utilities called the Berkeley Software Distribution. Then
they looked upon Version 7, and saw that it was better, and released the
Second Berkeley Software Distribution, and the Third, and then they merged
with a collateral descendent of Version 7 called 32V (and a lot of money from
the Department of Defense, who needed a standard OS for their new network),
and released the Fourth, and the world looked upon 4BSD and saw that it was
good. Which is where you came in, right... so we'll let that stand and go
back to see where System V comes in.

	I don't think there was a System IV, not publicly released.

	Anyway.

	AT&T (information systems, not Bell Labs) decided that System III
was good, but could be better, and released System V. They also sold a
whole bunch of computers with names like 3b1 (the computer with the future
built in) and 3b2 running it.

	And SCO looked upon System V and produced a series of System V
based releases called SCO UNIX and Open Desktop and Open Server, each more
complex and hard to understand than the last, with all manner of bundled
software and new and different programming models, until the last versions
of Open Server had half a dozen separate and unrelated sets of system
admin tools cobbled loosely together.

	And AT&T looked upon BSD and saw that it was good, and since Berkeley
was giving it away to anyone with a license (and AT&T certanly qualified) they
borrowed a bunch of stuff from 4BSD to create System V Release 2. This is
important later on.

	And AT&T looked upon what Bell Labs was doing, and merged a bunch
of it with SVR2 to produce SVR3, some time around the time Bell Labs was
producing the 8th Edition (Version 8, but I don't think it was ever released
that way). Dennis Ritchie was apparently unimpressed wit hthe way they did
this, by the way.

	All this time BSD is spinning off its own storm of variants.

	IBM looked upon SVR2 and 4.3, and brought them together with some
of their own code and produced something called AIX.

	AT&T looked upon SCO Open Whatever and, against all common sense,
decided that it was also good and merged SVR3 and SCO Open Thingy to
produce SVR2.3, which was surprisingly clean and un-broken, considering
what SCO's software looked like at the time.

	And now Sun decided that Syetem V was the OS with the future built
in, and set their 4BSD-based SunOS 4 aside so they could get together with
AT&T and SCO (again) to produce The Next Big Thing, System V Release V (for
AT&T) and Solaris (for Sun) which is basically SVR4 plus bits of SunOS and
a user interface model called OpenLook which is partly based on the academic
X-Windows system and Sun's own very interesting (and soon to be defunct)
Network Extensible Window System (NeWS). This was all under the name of UNIX
International.

	And everyone else went, "verily, this doth scare us, let us produce
our own Next Big Thing" and they took IBM's SVR3.2 source license, and some
version of 4.3, and code from AIX and Ultrix and this academic meta-OS
called Mach (which may or may not be a Microkernel, but it's a fact that
there's Mach developers over at Microsoft by this time working on their
Next Big thing called NT, which also may or may not be a Microkernel) and
produced a system called OSF-1, named after the Open Software Foundation.

	Open was a Big Word already.

	So we had SVR4/Solaris/OpenLook on one side, under UNIX International,
and BSD/OSF1/Motif on the other side, under the OSF (and a whole bunch of
people who just wanted to use BSD and thought this whole thing was stupid and
Motif was ugly and smelled funny). Meanwhile, Microsoft is sharpening Windows
and NT, and Bell Labs are doing very interesting things with distributed
computing systems that everyone ignored, which was a pity.

	OK, what happened next? Well, some people merged BSD and System V
by running two sets of APIs in the same system (Pyramid seems to have
done it best, but Solaris at least made an attempt). Others built various
compatibility layers... Digital UNIX was originally called OSF/1, but it was
really (as noted) 4.3-Reno and Mach with some System V system calls and
programs slapped on top. Personally, I rather liked it and I'm not at all
happy that Compaq and HP are talking about replacing it with HPUX.

	Steve Jobs took Mach and BSD and did his own thing with it, and
ended up with NeXTSTeP, with its own incompatible (but rather nice) network
window system that really should have paid more attention to NeWS than just
copying the capitalization and the idea of embedding Postscript in the
display server. This has, by a roundabout way, combined back with 4.4-Lite
(via FreeBSD) to produce Darwin, the UNIX OS layer under Apple's new OS X.

	Oh, and while I'm talking about 4.4-Lite, remember that bit back
there where AT&T grabbed some code from BSD? Well, that was important.
Because when the people at Berkeley decided to try and rip out the AT&T
code from BSD so they could give it away to anyone who wanted it (in,
to be precise, Net/2), AT&T (information systems, by now called Unix
Support Group) sued them.

	This would have been bad for BSD, and it did cause problems, but it
turns out when AT&T took that code from Berkeley they neglected to follow
the letter of Berkeley's license, and so AT&T and Berkeley ended up holding
loaded lawsuits to each others heads. And after about a year of wrangling,
they gently put their weapons down and backed away... and so 4.4 could move
forward.

	But all that took time, and in the meantime a clever fellow in
Finland and several thousand of his friends released this thing called
Linux.

	And off in another corner... Microsoft ships NT (which became
Windows NT, then Windows 2000, and Windows XP, as Microsoft figures
people just don't *care* about operating systems, and they seem to be
right about that), and Bill Gates calls NT the best version of UNIX
ever.

	And still other people decided that UNIX sucked, and Windows sucked,
and went off to do their own thing. Amiga, for example, and Be... both of
which now seem destined to end up in handheld computers. Odd, that.

	But in any case it seems that there's only one place that there's
really any deep competition in operating systems any more. And that's
places where you can't see the operating system at all. It's amazing
the different kinds of software you find in car engines, electronic
appliances, and factories. Compared to some of them, NT almost does
look like another UNIX variant. Especially considering all the BSD code
Microsoft ended up borrowing...

