I've corresponded with a Compaq engineer about ladebug vs. gdb in the
past (he said I'm one of a "select few" of ladebug's "vocal users" ;-) ).
He asked why people like gdb so much and why so few people use ladebug.
I gave him long detailed answers (summary: gdb has a cross-platform
interface, readline support beats dbx/ladebug command line editing, more
graphical front ends support gdb than ladebug, and ladebug and dbx require
a license).  I think he took my comments to his group for discussion.

In any case, he said that he had personally disabled the license check
in some version of the debugger (whether he was talking about ladebug
or dbx I'm not sure, but I think he was talking about dbx), and he also
said that the ladebug license check calls also are not taking place,
though Compaq is telling people they should have the license anyway, so that
in the future if they decide to really make ladebug require the license, they
can.

My guess is you just need to be using a more recent version of the operating
system.  You might even be able to download ladebug from Compaq's development
tools site and have it work on your version of the OS, depending on what your
version of the OS *is*.

Tim
