[ You probably can't quote any of this, but I did read the architecture
manuals. ]

I wish I knew why the Alpha chip went away.  To me it feels like someone found
the cure for cancer or discovered the method that Beethoven used to write his
final symphony even though he was completely deaf.  Astounding the number of
times that something truly fantastic and good has been thrown away because of
mis-management and greed.  I had worked with the Alpha chip for a while and
was astounded at its architecture and brute force number crunching speed.  My
background is heavily mixed with three parts floating point number crunching
and one part twelve year old scotch.   I had ported some code from my days in
the military doing ballistics and fluid flow computation to the IBM 3090
MainFrame in Fortran 4, C and some hand rolled assembly.  The same code ran on
a quad processor Dec Alpha 4100 ( minus the Assembly bits ) with about the
same speed.  From a 22 million dollar a year mainframe to the Dec Alpha server
at about a half million.  I was at Pratt & Whitney by the time the DEC Alpha
was showing some real strength in numerical computations.  We had migrated a
ton of VMS systems forward ( or sideways ) to AIX RS/6000,  Sun and SGI.  The
idea was to get off that expensive big blue box and move over to lesser
hardware.  Myself and a few other number crunchers were thrilled with the DEC
Alpha's ability to kill off massive computational tasks.   Eventually the cost
came down and we saw Linux ported to the DEC alpha along with NT 3.51.  I
think that NT 4.0 was ported to the Alpha but I never found a good reason to
cripple a 64-bit architecture with a 32-bit Solitaire game.  ( yeah .. there
is a lot of acid in my tone ).   Eventually Compaq bought out Digital and the
writing was on the wall once that Wintel organization got their hands on it. 
I couldn't see any top brass at Intel or Compaq meeting in agreement over the
furtherance of the competitive and massively superior 64-bit Alpha.   I still
go shopping on eBay from time to time looking for DEC Alpha workstations with
really fast processors just so I can keep one running Linux.   Mostly because
I ported a lot of source code from my thesis work over to the DEC Alpha and so
yes, I am biased.  Heavily.   Other than an IBM 3090 mainframe with a vector
processor option, I have never seen anything crunch numbers faster.  It was
fairly clear then why I drifted over to my other favorite, Sun Sparc and
UltraSparc.   The UltraSparc III+ is showing the signs of a world class number
cruncher as well, just as soon as the FP NAN Traps are sorted out.

If Microsoft, Compaq/HP and Intel had the cure for the common cold, they would
sell it and people would line up after watching nifty ads with lots of music
and flashy colors.  It wouldn't work but people would buy it.

I'm ranting again .... sorry about that.

Dennis Clarke
number cruncher ... in my spare time 

ps: If anyone has an interest in Markov processes or Stochastic dynamics
within chaotic systems then feel free to drop a line.


