You don't need to talk with your machine. ;-)
Instal your new disk in the box, set the SCSI-id jumpers correct and 
boot your machine.
Next steps are depend from your DUX/TRU64 Version:

Dig. Unix 4.0a to f (boot messages):
====================================
...                                       
 scsi0 at ata0 slot 0 
 rz0 at scsi0 target 0 lun 0 (LID=0) 
 _(TOSHIBA CD-ROM XM-5702B  3476)      # the CD-ROM drive on the IDE
channel
...
 scsi2 at isp0 slot 0 
 rz16 at scsi2 target 0 lun 0 (LID=1)  # the 1. SCSI drive
 _(DEC     RZ1CC-BA (C) DEC 880F) 
 _(Wide16) 
 rz17 at scsi2 target 1 lun 0 (LID=2)  # the 2nd. SCSI drive
 _(SEAGATE ST34572W         0784) 
 _(Wide16) 
 rz18 at scsi2 target 2 lun 0 (LID=3)  # and so on ...
 _(SEAGATE ST34572W         0784) 
 _(Wide16)
...

Unix is up an runnng, please log in as root and change to /dev
prompt> ./MAKEDEV rz17  
MAKEDEV: special file(s) for rz17: 
rz17a rrz17a rz17b rrz17b rz17c rrz17c rz17d rrz17d rz17e rrz17e 
rz17f rrz17f rz17g rrz17g rz17h rrz17h
prompt>
prompt> disklabel -wr /dev/rrz17a rzxx
than you can use the diskconfig command to create/modify
the default (rzxx) partitions layout.
prompt> diskconfig &
after this,prompt> newfs /dev/disk/rz17a (for ufs - filesystems)

... and so on.

TRU64 5.0, ... 5.1A:

install your new disk in the box, set the correct SCSI-id and boot
your UNIX. After the OS is running, log in as root and type:

prompt> hwmgr show scsi

        SCSI                DEVICE    DEVICE  DRIVER NUM  DEVICE FIRST 
 HWID:  DEVICEID HOSTNAME   TYPE      SUBTYPE OWNER  PATH FILE   VALID
PATH
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
   41:  0        asterix    disk      none    2      1    dsk0  
[0/0/0]   
   42:  1        asterix    disk      none    2      1    dsk1  
[0/1/0]   
   43:  2        asterix    disk      none    2      1    dsk2   [0/2/0]

prompt>

now you can create a disklabel by hand,

prompt> disklabel -wr /dev/rdisk/dsk1a rzxx (rzxx is a standard
partition table)

or use the "diskconfig" tool with a gui.



 
