In article <Pine.LNX.4.33.0203081257580.17688-100000@anahata.ma.ic.ac.uk> <andy.thomas@ic.ac.uk> writes:
>Running sys_check on my Tru64 5.1 system reports that the vm-maxvas kernel
>parameter needs to be increased. What command/utility does sys_scheck use
>to find the value of this attribute? sysconfig -q vm doesn't list this
>attribute at all and I'm vary of using sysconfigdb to add it into my
>/etc/sysconfigtab (where it doesn't exist either).

The vm tunable maxvas was replaced (a long time ago) by the user
resource limit RLIMIT_AS. See the getrlimit/setrlimit (2) man pages.
There are limiting tunables in the proc subsystem:
  # sysconfig -q proc | grep proc
  proc:
  max_proc_per_user = 256
  per_proc_stack_size = 8388608
  max_per_proc_stack_size = 33554432
  per_proc_data_size = 1342177280
  max_per_proc_data_size = 1073741824
  max_per_proc_address_space = 4294967296
  per_proc_address_space = 4294967296

I'm surprised that sys_check still emits this information; do
you have the latest version?

>Also is it 'vm_maxvas' (as given in the System Configuration & Tuning
>book) or 'vm-maxvas' (as given by sys_check's HTML output)?

Either one would work. The new "politically correct" format for tunables
is with underscores (as in: vm_maxvas). But the configuration subsystem
will match formats for backward compatibility. Of course, this tunable
doesn't exist any more :-)

