I work with various UNIX flavours, primarily Solaris and AIX but use HP and
Tru64 in a large datacentre.

I would say my favourite is AIX (because that's what I learnt on!! <G>) but
it also has excellent disk management, print management and package/software
manipulation - far beyond Solaris and Tru64 IMHO.

HP-UX is very similar to AIX. Trouble is that both these OS/s stray from the
standard UNIX way of doing things, i.e. Solaris places mount information in
/etc/vfstab whereas AIX does it it /etc/filesystems. Solaris uses the
standrard /etc/rcx.d to start daemons, but AIX uses primarily /etc/inittab.
AIX also uses a SRCMaster Daemon to control other daemons like inetd so to
stop inetd in AIX, you'd type 'stopsrc -s inetd, or refresh -s inetd'. In
Solaris you use the kill -HUP <PID of Inetd> - little differences but enough
to throw you.

I like Solaris because it is well supported and appears to run faster than
both AIX and HP-UX on similar hardware. The disk management of Solaris (and
Tru-64) is rubbish (again IMHO) but you can buy add-ons. Disksuite is basic
but free and Volume Manager is much more powerful but still no-where as good
as AIX disk management (which is free).

There you go. I would argue - if you are going to be using a smallish
server, go for Solaris, but you are going Enterprise (> 8 processors, >100Gb
disk), either go for AIX or Solaris. Why didn't I recommend HP or Tru-64?
I'm sure they have their strong points, but I'm not that up on those two
OS/s.

Karhu



