4Front Technologies wrote:

> I am trying to compile an application that uses pthread.h and libpthread.a
> and I need to know where to find "posix" compatible pthreads?. I only see
> dce compatible pthread.h in /usr/include/dce. Unfortunately this app
> doesn't like dce pthreads.

What version of Tru64 UNIX are you using? One might infer from your use of 
the term "tru64" that you're using something relatively recent, but it's 
hard to be sure.

If you should chance to actually be using DEC OSF/1 3.x, then you need to 
upgrade -- it didn't have POSIX threads. The standard only became final as 
we were developing Digital UNIX V4.0.

If you're on V4.0x or later, then you'll find the POSIX thread header file 
precisely where it must be, in /usr/include/pthread.h. (#include 
<pthread.h>.) The DCE thread header files remain in /usr/include/dce/ but 
you really shouldn't be using them. (Certainly not if you want POSIX 
threads.)

V4.0x, however, lacked a supported libpthread.a. You should be using the 
shared library, /usr/shlib/libpthread.so. If you really needed, for one or 
another bizarre reason, to use a static archive, then you can install an 
optional development kit (I don't remember the name and won't bother to 
look it up right now), but beware that it won't continue to work on newer 
versions or necessarily even patch kits.

This changed, due to popular demand, with the release of Tru64 UNIX V5.0, 
which does have a supported libpthread.a on the standard kit. You still 
shoudln't use it, though. The shared library is better. With it you get 
full development support (the debugger and other tools like Visual Threads 
can't fully support threaded programs built against the static archive). 
Also, despite the requirement for continued binary compatibility with old 
static images, the private kernel interfaces used by the thread library do 
change now and then, and you'll lose efficiency (and sometimes the full 
effect of bug fixes) if you're using an old static.

