Well, this is strictly from UNIX Network Programming Volume 1, Second
Edition,  Networking APIs: Sockets and XTI by W. Richard Stevens.  Section
1.8 BSD Networking History : 

4.2 BSD ( 1983 )  ->  4.3 BSD ( 1986 ) -> 4.3 BSD Tahoe ( 1988 )

The 4.3 BSD Tahoe release spawned two derivitives : 
    
   1)  BSD Networking Software Release 1.0 (1989) : Net/1
   2)  4.3 BSD Reno ( 1990 ) which seems to be a natural 
       progression of the BSD implementation
  
From the 4.3 BSD Reno we see another revision of BSD Networking Software
Release 2.0 (1991) : Net/2.

4.4 BSD ( 1993 ) resulted in two flavours of BSD Lite : 

   1)  4.4 BSD-Lite (1994) referred to as Net/3 
   2)  4.4 BSD-Lite2 (1995)

These last two BSD-Lite brothers seem to be the basis of the following
common BSD flavours : 

   1)  BSD/OS
   2)  FreeBSD
   3)  NetBSD
   4)  OpenBSD

Now where do the SVR4 flavours fit in?  I need another book and I think
I'll pull one from the extreme other end of the technical gene pool!

From UNIX for Dummies, second Edition ( like we needed two! )
Chapter 2, What is UNIX, Anyway?

As Diverse as Bacteria in a Cesspool
------------------------------------
Indulge us as we tell a hstorical parable.  Imagine, for the moment, that
UNIX is a kind of automobile rather than a computer system.  in the early
days, every UNIX system was distributed with a complete set of source code
and development tools.  If UNIX had been a car, this distribution method
would have been the sam as every car being supplied with a complete set of
blueprints, wrenches, arc-welders, and other car-building tools. Now
imagine that nearly all these cars were sold to engineering schools.  You
might expect that the students would get to work on their cars and, pretty
soon, no two cars would be the same.  That's pretty much what happened to
UNIX.
------------------------------------

Well, that's what is written there and I felt it was reasonable to type it
in here.  A far better answer to the source of out AT&T Bell Labs derived
System Five Release four inspired current Sun Solaris would be found at
the Bell Labs site http://www.bell-labs.com/history/unix/ .  That page has
a little picture on the bottom of the old childrens building blocks that
spell UNIX.  I remember seeing that original picture in an old copy of
Compute way back when building a kit computer from Heathkit was still
great fun.

The original SunOS, I believe,  based on rumour superstition and coffee
whispers, was the Stanford University Network ( SUN ) implementation of a
BSD derivitive.  SunOS was a BSD flavour of UNIX for quite a while before
we had Solaris.  Solaris was a SVR4 derivitive and that is what we still
have.  I am sure that there are other regulars in comp.unix.solaris that
can fill in details of how we got here from way back there.  I certainly
hope that someone will.

In any case, this has been a long post and so I'll take a break from it
because I may not have clarified anything but had fun while doing it.

