In article <3C7E2798.70608@usa.net>,
Ivan Dolezal  <Ivan.Dolezal@usa.net> wrote:
> Btw: what is, guys, your opinion?

I'm probably a bit of an oddball here. I don't much see the point in
going to any great effort to run free UNIX on non-intel hardware.
There's a couple of situations where it's a good idea, but in most
cases you can get a discarded wintel box that'll run a much more stable
and well-supported BSD/Linux port faster than just about any non-intel
hardware.

The main reasons where it's worth the effort are:

	1. You're doing it so you can support the port effort.

	2. Your non-wintel box is too old to be any use running the
	   vendor's OS.

	3. Your non-wintel box is faster/bigger/more powerful/more
	   reliable than a wintel box.

	4. Your non-wintel box has some other capability none of the
	   wintel boxes at hand have.

I run OpenBSD on Sparcstations, for example, for reasons 2 and 4. IBM sells
servers that satisfy reason 3, and newer Alphas certainly qualify there as
well... but if you're not talking about an ES4x or the like I suspect that's
not your motivation.

Of course reason 4 can be stretched to include "cool factor"... :->

But most of the time, you're better off getting a discarded wintel box and
a KVM switch and running your BSD fix on that, since it'll give you all the
capability of the non-x86 hardware and you can still run stuff that works on
the native OS.

I'm particularly puzzled, on this front, by the people who run BSD and Linux
on iMacs. :->

